examples:
  - id: CONV-001
    domain: pricing
    source_books:
      - Predictably Irrational — Dan Ariely
      - Thinking, Fast and Slow — Daniel Kahneman
      - Priceless — William Poundstone
    cluster_segments:
      - source_book: Predictably Irrational — Dan Ariely
        text: "The Economist's subscription page became a famous case study. They offered three options:\nWeb-only for $59,
          Print-only for $125, and Print+Web for $125. The Print-only option was\nthe decoy — nobody chose it, but its presence
          made Print+Web look like a bargain. Dan Ariely\ntested this with his students: when the decoy was present, 84% chose
          Print+Web. When he\nremoved the decoy and offered only Web ($59) and Print+Web ($125), only 32% chose Print+Web.\n"
      - source_book: Thinking, Fast and Slow — Daniel Kahneman
        text: "The dominance relationship makes choices easier. When A dominates B in all respects, people\nchoose A without
          hesitation. But when A is better than B on some dimensions and worse on\nothers, choice becomes difficult and people
          often defer. This is why adding a clearly inferior\noption that is dominated by the target can increase the target's
          share dramatically.\n"
      - source_book: Priceless — William Poundstone
        text: "Williams-Sonoma sold a bread maker for $279 — it flopped. Then they introduced a larger,\ndeluxe model for
          $429. The $279 model suddenly looked reasonable and sales nearly doubled.\nThe expensive model wasn't meant to sell;
          it was there to make the original look like a\ngood deal. This is asymmetric dominance at work in retail.\n"
    is_convergent: true
    should_extract: true
    expected_fb:
      is_summary: false
      route: FB
      name: Asymmetric Dominance Decoy
      definition: "Introducing an inferior decoy option that is dominated by a target option on all salient\ndimensions shifts
        choice toward the target by making its relative value obvious. The decoy\nis not meant to be chosen — it exists solely
        to make the target appear superior through\ncontrast, exploiting the human preference for easy dominance comparisons
        over difficult\ntrade-off evaluations.\n"
      mechanism: "Decoys cause preference shifts because System 1 (intuitive) reasoning resolves dominance\ncomparisons effortlessly,
        while trade-off comparisons between non-dominated options trigger\nSystem 2 (deliberative) reasoning that often results
        in choice deferral. The decoy\neliminates the need for difficult trade-off analysis by providing an obvious loser.\n"
      boundary: "Applies when the decoy is clearly inferior to the target on all relevant dimensions AND\nthe target and competitor
        are difficult to compare directly (each has advantages). Fails\nwhen: (1) the decoy is not clearly dominated — if
        it has ANY advantage over the target,\nit becomes a legitimate option; (2) the target and competitor are already easily
        comparable\n— no need for a decoy; (3) consumers have strong pre-existing preferences that overwhelm\ncontextual cues.\n"
      consequence: "Markets structured with decoy options produce systematically different choices than markets\nwith only
        genuinely competitive options. This means the same consumer with the same true\npreferences will make different choices
        depending on whether a decoy is present — the\npreference is constructed at the moment of choice, not revealed by
        it.\n"
      evidence_passages:
        - when the decoy was present, 84% chose Print+Web
        - When he removed the decoy and offered only Web ($59) and Print+Web ($125), only 32% chose Print+Web
        - adding a clearly inferior option that is dominated by the target can increase the target's share dramatically
        - Williams-Sonoma sold a bread maker for $279 — it flopped. Then they introduced a larger, deluxe model for 
          $429. The $279 model suddenly looked reasonable and sales nearly doubled
    rationale: "Three independent sources (Ariely, Kahneman, Poundstone) with three different empirical cases\n(Economist
      subscriptions, general dominance logic, Williams-Sonoma bread makers) converging\non the same mechanism. Convergent
      extraction MUST synthesize across all sources, not paraphrase\nany single one. The mechanism/boundary/consequence structure
      forces the LLM to go beyond\nobservation (\"decoys work\") to explanation (\"they work because...\") and limits (\"\
      they fail when...\").\n"
  - id: CONV-002
    domain: behavioral_change
    source_books:
      - Atomic Habits — James Clear
      - The Power of Habit — Charles Duhigg
    cluster_segments:
      - source_book: Atomic Habits — James Clear
        text: "Implementation intentions leverage the two most common cues: time and location. The format\nis 'I will [BEHAVIOR]
          at [TIME] in [LOCATION].' For example: 'I will meditate for one minute\nat 7 a.m. in my kitchen.' Research by Peter
          Gollwitzer has shown that people who use\nimplementation intentions are two to three times more likely to follow
          through. The specificity\nof the plan creates an automatic trigger — when the time and place arrive, the behavior\n\
          is initiated without conscious deliberation.\n"
      - source_book: The Power of Habit — Charles Duhigg
        text: "The cue-routine-reward loop explains why some behaviors become automatic while others require\nconstant effort.
          The cue is the trigger that tells your brain to go into automatic mode.\nImplementation intentions work because
          they manufacture a clear, unambiguous cue. When you say\n'I will exercise at 6 p.m. in the park,' the clock striking
          six becomes a cue that initiates\nthe routine without requiring a decision.\n"
      - source_book: Atomic Habits — James Clear
        text: "The problem with vague goals like 'I want to exercise more' is that they leave too many\ndecisions unmade.
          Each day you must decide when, where, and how to exercise. Each decision\nis an opportunity to opt out. Implementation
          intentions remove these decision points by\npre-committing to a specific plan. When the moment arrives, there is
          nothing to decide.\n"
    is_convergent: true
    should_extract: true
    expected_fb:
      is_summary: false
      route: FB
      name: Implementation Intention Triggering
      definition: "Specifying the exact time, location, and behavioral format of an intended action ('I will\n[BEHAVIOR] at
        [TIME] in [LOCATION]') increases follow-through 2-3x by converting vague\nintentions into automatic cue-response triggers.
        The specificity eliminates the decision\npoints where willpower typically fails.\n"
      mechanism: "Implementation intentions cause behavior follow-through because they manufacture a clear,\nunambiguous situational
        cue (time + location) that triggers automatic initiation of the\nplanned behavior without requiring conscious deliberation.
        This bypasses the decision-fatigue\nbottleneck where vague intentions break down — when the specified time and place
        arrive,\nthe pre-committed plan executes as if on autopilot.\n"
      boundary: "Applies when the target behavior is simple, specific, and the cue is unambiguous (e.g.,\n'meditate at 7am
        in the kitchen'). Fails when: (1) the behavior is complex and cannot be\nreduced to a simple trigger (e.g., 'write
        a novel at 9am' — the behavior itself requires\nongoing decisions); (2) the cue is frequently unpredictable (e.g.,
        'exercise when I feel\nenergized'); (3) the person has no genuine motivation — implementation intentions amplify\n\
        existing motivation, they do not create it.\n"
      consequence: "People who use implementation intentions are 2-3x more likely to follow through compared to\nthose with
        equivalent motivation but vague plans. The effect compounds: each successful\nautomatic execution reinforces the cue-response
        link, making future execution even more likely.\n"
      evidence_passages:
        - people who use implementation intentions are two to three times more likely to follow through
        - Implementation intentions work because they manufacture a clear, unambiguous cue
        - implementation intentions remove these decision points by pre-committing to a specific plan
    rationale: "Two sources (Clear and Duhigg) converging on the same mechanism with complementary explanations.\nClear provides
      the specificity + Gollwitzer research data. Duhigg provides the neurological\nexplanation (cue-routine-reward loop).
      A good convergent extraction synthesizes both: the\n\"what\" from Clear + the \"why\" from Duhigg.\n"
  - id: CONV-003
    domain: advertising
    source_books:
      - Finding the Tipping Point — Visual Metaphor in Advertising
      - The Cognitive Effect of Visual Metaphor in Advertising — Mostafa
    cluster_segments:
      - source_book: Finding the Tipping Point — Visual Metaphor in Advertising
        text: "Visual metaphors in advertising follow an inverted-U relationship with conceptual complexity.\nModerately complex
          metaphors — those requiring some cognitive effort to resolve but not so\nmuch that they frustrate — produce the
          most favorable attitudes toward both the ad and the\nbrand. When a metaphor is too obvious, viewers dismiss it as
          trivial. When it is too obscure,\nthey disengage. The sweet spot is the 'aha' moment that occurs when the viewer
          successfully\nresolves the metaphor.\n"
      - source_book: The Cognitive Effect of Visual Metaphor in Advertising — Mostafa
        text: "Our experimental results confirm a curvilinear relationship between metaphor complexity\nand comprehension.
          At low complexity (literal juxtapositions), processing fluency is high\nbut elaboration is low — viewers 'get it'
          immediately but don't think about it. At high\ncomplexity (abstract conceptual blends), comprehension drops sharply:
          only 23% of\nparticipants correctly interpreted the intended meaning. At moderate complexity, both\ncomprehension
          (87%) and elaboration are maximized.\n"
    is_convergent: true
    should_extract: true
    expected_fb:
      is_summary: false
      route: FB
      name: Metaphor Complexity Inverted-U
      definition: "Visual metaphors in advertising have a curvilinear (inverted-U) relationship with persuasive\nimpact: moderate
        conceptual complexity produces the strongest brand attitudes and message\nelaboration, while too-simple metaphors
        are dismissed as trivial and too-complex metaphors\ncause disengagement or misinterpretation. The optimal level triggers
        an 'aha' resolution\nmoment that rewards the viewer's cognitive effort.\n"
      mechanism: "Moderate metaphor complexity causes stronger persuasion because it triggers elaborative\nprocessing — the
        viewer invests cognitive effort to resolve the metaphor and is rewarded\nwith an 'aha' insight. This self-generated
        insight is more memorable and persuasive than\na message that is simply received passively. Too little complexity
        provides no reward;\ntoo much complexity prevents resolution entirely.\n"
      boundary: "Applies when the audience has sufficient domain knowledge to resolve the metaphor (cultural\nreferences,
        category familiarity). Fails when: (1) the audience lacks prerequisite knowledge\n— a moderately complex metaphor
        becomes too complex; (2) the medium limits processing time\n(e.g., billboards vs. magazine ads); (3) the product category
        is high-involvement where\nliteral information is preferred over metaphorical.\n"
      consequence: "Ads with moderately complex metaphors generate higher recall, more favorable brand attitudes,\nand greater
        message elaboration than either literal or highly abstract ads. The processing\neffort itself becomes a persuasive
        force — we value insights we work for more than those\nwe receive passively.\n"
      evidence_passages:
        - Moderately complex metaphors [...] produce the most favorable attitudes toward both the ad and the brand
        - At moderate complexity, both comprehension (87%) and elaboration are maximized
        - The sweet spot is the 'aha' moment that occurs when the viewer successfully resolves the metaphor
    rationale: "Two sources from the same academic domain converging on the inverted-U finding — one providing\nthe theoretical
      framework (tipping point, 'aha' moment), the other providing experimental data\n(87% comprehension at moderate, 23%
      at high). This is a convergent FB from our actual pipeline\ndata (cluster_271).\n"
  - id: CONV-004
    domain: persuasion
    source_books:
      - Influence — Robert Cialdini
      - Made to Stick — Chip & Dan Heath
    cluster_segments:
      - source_book: Influence — Robert Cialdini
        text: "Social proof is most effective when the proof comes from similar others. Showing that '10,000\npeople bought
          this' is less persuasive than showing that 'people like you bought this.' The\neffect is strongest when people are
          uncertain about what to do — in ambiguous situations, we\nlook to others' behavior as a heuristic for correct action.\n"
      - source_book: Made to Stick — Chip & Dan Heath
        text: "NFL Films tells the story of an undrafted rookie who made the team. Rather than listing\nstatistics, they show
          veterans talking about 'that kid who reminds me of myself.' The\nsimilarity between the viewer (who sees themselves
          as overlooked) and the rookie creates\nemotional resonance that raw achievement data cannot. The principle: people
          believe stories\nabout people who remind them of themselves.\n"
    is_convergent: true
    should_extract: true
    expected_fb:
      is_summary: false
      route: FB
      name: Similarity-Weighted Social Proof
      definition: "Social proof persuades through perceived similarity, not volume. A message showing that\n'people like you'
        took an action is more influential than showing that '10,000 people' took\nit, because relevance amplifies the heuristic.
        The effect is strongest under uncertainty —\nwhen people don't know what to do, they look to similar others as a decision
        shortcut.\n"
      mechanism: "Similarity-weighted social proof causes persuasion because it activates self-relevant\nprocessing — the
        viewer sees the referenced group as a proxy for themselves ('if they did\nit, I should too'). Volume-based proof (10,000
        people) activates statistical reasoning,\nwhich is weaker than identity-based reasoning. The mechanism is amplified
        by uncertainty:\nthe less someone knows what to do, the more they rely on similar-other behavior as a\ndecision heuristic.\n"
      boundary: "Applies when the reference group shares a relevant, visible identity characteristic with\nthe target audience.
        Fails when: (1) the similarity is on irrelevant dimensions — 'people\nwho drink coffee bought this' doesn't persuade
        if the decision has nothing to do with\ncoffee drinking; (2) the reference group's behavior is undesirable — similarity
        backfires\nif the referenced behavior is stigmatized; (3) the audience has strong independent\npreferences that override
        social cues.\n"
      consequence: "Social proof campaigns that emphasize 'people like you' consistently outperform volume-based\ncampaigns
        ('X million served') in conversion and engagement. The identity connection creates\nan emotional resonance that statistical
        proof cannot match.\n"
      evidence_passages:
        - Showing that '10,000 people bought this' is less persuasive than showing that 'people like you bought this.'
        - people believe stories about people who remind them of themselves
        - the effect is strongest when people are uncertain about what to do
    rationale: "Cialdini provides the mechanism (similarity amplifies social proof under uncertainty).\nHeath provides the
      narrative application (NFL Films example). Convergent extraction must\nsynthesize the mechanism with the application,
      not just paraphrase Cialdini.\n"
  - id: CONV-005
    domain: marketing
    source_books:
      - Hacking Growth — Sean Ellis
      - $100M Offers — Alex Hormozi
    cluster_segments:
      - source_book: Hacking Growth — Sean Ellis
        text: "When Dropbox launched, Drew Houston created a 3-minute demo video showing the product in\naction — syncing
          files across devices, recovering deleted files, sharing folders. The video\ndemonstrated the outcome before mentioning
          price or features. Beta signups jumped from\n5,000 to 75,000 overnight.\n"
      - source_book: $100M Offers — Alex Hormozi
        text: "The grand slam offer is not about listing features. It is about making the prospect feel\nthe outcome so vividly
          that the price becomes an afterthought. You must articulate the\ntransformation — where they are now versus where
          they will be after using your product —\nwith such specificity that they experience the relief of the problem being
          solved before\nthey pay a cent.\n"
    is_convergent: true
    should_extract: true
    expected_fb:
      is_summary: false
      route: FB
      name: Value-First Demonstration
      definition: "Leading with the experienced outcome of a product — rather than its features, specifications,\nor price
        — increases conversion by allowing prospects to feel the transformation before\nevaluating the cost. The value demonstration
        makes the price an afterthought rather than a\nbarrier, because the prospect has already internalized the benefit.\n"
      mechanism: "Value-first demonstration causes higher conversion because outcome visualization\ncreates a vivid internal
        reference point — prospects imagine the transformation so\nconcretely that the price is evaluated against the relief
        of the solved problem rather\nthan against competitor feature lists. When the prospect has already experienced the\n\
        outcome in their mind, the price becomes the cost of not achieving that outcome, not\nthe cost of the product.\n"
      boundary: "Applies when the product delivers a clear, emotionally resonant transformation (productivity,\nstatus, relief,
        connection). Fails when: (1) the product is a commodity where all competitors\ndeliver equivalent outcomes — value
        demonstration can't differentiate; (2) the audience\nalready understands the outcome and is evaluating features/price
        — value-first becomes\nredundant; (3) the product requires explanation of how it works before prospects trust the\n\
        outcome claim.\n"
      consequence: "Products marketed with value-first demonstration consistently outperform feature-first\nmarketing on conversion
        metrics. Dropbox's video approach (5K→75K signups) is the canonical\nexample: showing the outcome created demand that
        feature lists never generated.\n"
      evidence_passages:
        - Beta signups jumped from 5,000 to 75,000 overnight
        - making the prospect feel the outcome so vividly that the price becomes an afterthought
        - they experience the relief of the problem being solved before they pay a cent
    rationale: "Ellis provides the case study (Dropbox, 5K→75K). Hormozi provides the mechanism\n(outcome visualization, price
      as afterthought). Convergent extraction must combine\nthe empirical evidence with the source-grounded mechanism.\n"
  - id: NEG-CONV-001
    domain: pricing
    source_books:
      - Predictably Irrational — Dan Ariely
    cluster_segments:
      - source_book: Predictably Irrational — Dan Ariely
        text: "When we encounter a new product, the first price we see becomes an anchor. This initial\nprice influences not
          just what we're willing to pay for that product, but what we're willing\nto pay for related products in the same
          category. Arbitrary anchors can have effects that\npersist even when people know the anchor was arbitrary.\n"
    is_convergent: false
    should_extract: false
    expected_fb:
      is_summary: false
      route: 'NULL'
      name: Anchoring
      definition: ''
      mechanism: ''
      boundary: ''
      consequence: ''
      evidence_passages: []
    rationale: "HARD NEGATIVE: This is a single-source segment passed as a \"cluster\" with only ONE book.\nStage 2 should
      detect that source_diversity < 2 and route this to non-convergent path\n(no extraction, or extract with is_convergent=false
      and flag for single-source verification).\nThe golden example trains the LLM to recognize when it does NOT have enough
      sources for\nconvergent synthesis.\n"
  - id: NEG-CONV-002
    domain: business
    source_books:
      - Good to Great — Jim Collins
      - Built to Last — Jim Collins
    cluster_segments:
      - source_book: Good to Great — Jim Collins
        text: "Great companies have great people. The right people are the most important asset of any\norganization. Getting
          the right people on the bus and in the right seats is the first\nstep to building a great company.\n"
      - source_book: Built to Last — Jim Collins
        text: "Visionary companies prioritize people development. They invest heavily in training and\ncreate cultures where
          talented people can thrive and grow. The commitment to people\nis what separates the truly great companies from
          the merely good ones.\n"
    is_convergent: true
    should_extract: false
    expected_fb:
      is_summary: false
      route: 'NULL'
      name: ''
      definition: ''
      mechanism: ''
      boundary: ''
      consequence: ''
      evidence_passages: []
    rationale: "HARD NEGATIVE: Both segments say \"good people are important\" in different words. This is\na platitude, not
      a principle. There is no mechanism (HOW do great people cause great\noutcomes?), no boundary (when does great talent
      NOT help?), no testable consequence. All\nsegments agree, but they agree on a truism. Stage 2 should detect the absence
      of causal\nmechanism and reject this cluster. Even though there are 2 sources, having multiple sources\nechoing the
      same platitude does not create a convergent principle.\n\nNOTE: Collins' \"First Who, Then What\" IS a defended principle
      with a mechanism in Good to\nGreat (Chapter 3: rigorous people decisions → right people self-motivate → no need for\n\
      heavy management). However, the EXCERPTS selected for this cluster deliberately omit that\nmechanism. This teaches the
      LLM to evaluate the cluster it RECEIVES — not the books it\nrecognizes — and reject when the provided segments lack
      causal depth, even if the source\nmaterial contains it elsewhere.\n"
  - id: NEG-CONV-003
    domain: management
    source_books:
      - Thanks for the Feedback — Douglas Stone & Sheila Heen
      - Reinventing Organizations — Frederic Laloux
    cluster_segments:
      - source_book: Thanks for the Feedback — Douglas Stone & Sheila Heen
        text: "Receiving feedback triggers one of three reactions: truth-triggers (is this accurate?), relationship-triggers
          (what does this say about our relationship?), and identity-triggers (what does this say about who I am?). The key
          to receiving feedback well is recognizing which trigger is firing and separating the message from the emotional
          reaction. Even well-intentioned feedback can provoke defensiveness if it threatens the recipient's sense of competence
          or belonging."
      - source_book: Reinventing Organizations — Frederic Laloux
        text: Self-managing organizations replace hierarchical oversight with peer-based feedback loops. Instead of a 
          manager conducting annual reviews, colleagues give real-time feedback through structured processes like the 
          "advice process" — anyone can make a decision, but must first seek advice from affected parties and 
          subject-matter experts. This distributes feedback across the organization and makes it a continuous, 
          operational function rather than a periodic, evaluative one.
    is_convergent: true
    should_extract: false
    expected_fb:
      is_summary: false
      route: 'NULL'
      name: ''
      definition: ''
      mechanism: ''
      boundary: ''
      consequence: ''
      evidence_passages: []
    rationale: 'HARD NEGATIVE — FALSE CONVERGENCE. Both segments use the word "feedback" and discuss its role in organizations,
      creating a strong topical overlap. But they operate at completely different levels of analysis: Stone & Heen address
      interpersonal psychology (emotional triggers, identity threat, individual defensiveness), while Laloux addresses organizational
      structure (peer processes, distributed decision-making, system-level feedback loops). There is NO shared causal mechanism
      — one says "feedback fails because of emotional triggers" and the other says "feedback works when structured as an organizational
      process." Merging these would create a false synthesis. Stage 2 must detect that topical overlap without causal convergence
      = NULL. This is more challenging than single-source rejection (NEG-CONV-001) or platitude detection (NEG-CONV-002) because
      both sources are legitimate and well-argued — they just describe different mechanisms at different levels, making this
      the most common real-world false-convergence pattern.'
  - id: CONV-006
    domain: economics
    source_books:
      - Thinking, Fast and Slow — Daniel Kahneman
      - Misbehaving — Richard Thaler
      - Predictably Irrational — Dan Ariely
    cluster_segments:
      - source_book: Thinking, Fast and Slow — Daniel Kahneman
        text: The default option is the one that will be chosen if the decision-maker does nothing. Defaults are 
          powerful because opting out requires an active decision, which engages System 2. When people are uncertain or 
          the choice is complex, they tend to stay with the default — not because they prefer it, but because changing 
          it costs mental effort. This is why organ donation rates are above 90% in countries with opt-out defaults and 
          below 15% in countries with opt-in defaults, despite similar underlying preferences.
      - source_book: Misbehaving — Richard Thaler
        text: 'Choice architecture is the design of the environment in which people make decisions. A nudge is any aspect
          of the choice architecture that alters behavior without forbidding options or changing economic incentives. Automatic
          enrollment in retirement savings plans — where employees are enrolled by default but can opt out — increases participation
          from roughly 40% to over 90%. The power of defaults comes not from coercion but from inertia: people tend to stick
          with whatever option requires no action.'
      - source_book: Predictably Irrational — Dan Ariely
        text: When people face too many options, they often fail to choose at all. In one experiment, shoppers presented
          with 24 jam varieties were far less likely to buy than those shown only 6 varieties — despite being more 
          attracted to the larger display. The cognitive cost of evaluating many options exceeds the benefit of having 
          more choices. Choice, beyond a certain point, becomes paralyzing rather than liberating.
      - source_book: Thinking, Fast and Slow — Daniel Kahneman
        text: When faced with a difficult question, System 1 often substitutes an easier one without the person 
          noticing. This is why people who are asked "how happy are you with your life?" before being asked "how many 
          dates have you had recently?" show no correlation between the two, but when asked in the reverse order, the 
          correlation jumps to 0.66. Complex evaluations are frequently replaced by simpler proxies that happen to be 
          accessible.
    is_convergent: true
    should_extract: true
    expected_fb:
      - is_summary: false
        route: FB
        name: Default Inertia Effect
        definition: Pre-selected default options dramatically influence outcomes because opting out requires active 
          cognitive effort. Participation rates shift from ~40% to 90%+ when enrollment is automatic rather than opt-in 
          — not because preferences change, but because the path of least resistance exploits decision inertia. The 
          effect is strongest when the decision is complex or the chooser is uncertain.
        mechanism: "Defaults cause behavior shifts because the status quo requires no action, while changing it activates
          System 2 deliberation. Opting out demands evaluating alternatives, weighing trade-offs, and executing a non-default
          action — each step is a friction point where inertia wins. The mechanism is amplified by uncertainty: when people
          don't know which option is best, staying with the default feels safer than risking an active choice that might be
          wrong."
        boundary: 'Applies when the default is plausibly acceptable and the decision is not emotionally charged. Fails when:
          (1) the default is clearly harmful — people will overcome inertia to avoid pain; (2) the decision is simple and
          low-stakes — the cognitive cost of switching is negligible; (3) strong pre-existing preferences exist — someone
          who has already decided will ignore the default.'
        consequence: 'Default architecture produces 50+ percentage-point swings in participation rates (organ donation: >90%
          vs <15%; retirement savings: >90% vs ~40%). Countries and companies that use opt-out defaults systematically achieve
          higher enrollment in socially beneficial programs without changing incentives or restricting choice.'
        evidence_passages:
          - organ donation rates are above 90% in countries with opt-out defaults and below 15% in countries with opt-in
            defaults
          - Automatic enrollment in retirement savings plans — where employees are enrolled by default but can opt out —
            increases participation from roughly 40% to over 90%
          - Defaults are powerful because opting out requires an active decision
      - is_summary: false
        route: FB
        name: Choice Overload Paralysis
        definition: Increasing the number of options beyond a moderate threshold reduces the likelihood of any choice 
          being made. Decision-makers are attracted to larger choice sets but become paralyzed when the cognitive cost 
          of evaluating trade-offs exceeds the expected benefit of finding a better option. The result is choice 
          deferral — doing nothing rather than risking a suboptimal selection.
        mechanism: 'Choice overload causes paralysis because each additional option increases the cognitive load of comparison
          exponentially, while the marginal benefit of having one more option diminishes. System 1 avoids this cost through
          attribute substitution: instead of evaluating all options, it substitutes an easier proxy judgment. When no easy
          proxy exists, the decision is deferred. The mechanism is self-reinforcing: each deferred choice makes future choices
          harder by adding unresolved options to the mental queue.'
        boundary: "Applies when options are difficult to compare (each has unique advantages) and the decision is one-shot
          (no trial-and-error). Fails when: (1) options are easily ranked on a single dimension — more is always better; (2)
          the decision-maker has strong pre-existing preferences that eliminate most options immediately; (3) the choice can
          be reversed at low cost — trialability eliminates paralysis because wrong choices aren't permanent."
        consequence: 'Consumers presented with 24 options buy at significantly lower rates than those presented with 6, despite
          reporting higher satisfaction with the larger selection. This has practical implications for product line design,
          menu engineering, and any domain where the provider controls the size of the choice set: fewer curated options often
          produce more decisions than exhaustive catalogs.'
        evidence_passages:
          - shoppers presented with 24 jam varieties were far less likely to buy than those shown only 6 varieties
          - When faced with a difficult question, System 1 often substitutes an easier one without the person noticing
          - Complex evaluations are frequently replaced by simpler proxies that happen to be accessible
    rationale: 1:N extraction where BOTH principles are genuinely cross-source. FB1 (Default Inertia Effect) synthesizes
      Kahneman's System 2 opt-out cost with Thaler's retirement-savings nudge data — 2 distinct sources. FB2 (Choice 
      Overload Paralysis) synthesizes Ariely's jam experiment with Kahneman's attribute substitution mechanism — 2 
      distinct sources. Each FB draws evidence from 2 independent books. The LLM returns a JSON array of 2 principle 
      objects. This example teaches that 1:N extraction requires EACH principle to be convergent — not just that the 
      cluster has multiple segments. Essential training for the split-probe gate (discover_principles).
  - id: CONV-007
    domain: economics
    source_books:
      - Algorithms to Live By — Brian Christian & Tom Griffiths
      - The Art of Strategy — Avinash Dixit & Barry Nalebuff
      - 'The Great Mental Models, Volume 4: Economics and Art — Shane Parrish'
    cluster_segments:
      - source_book: The Art of Strategy — Avinash Dixit & Barry Nalebuff
        text: "Keynes used the beauty contest as a metaphor for the stock market, where each investor\nwants to buy the stocks
          that will rise in price, which means the stocks that investors,\nin general, think will appreciate. The value of
          a stock is not what YOU think it's worth,\nbut what you think OTHERS think it's worth — and what they think others
          think, in an\ninfinite recursion of mutual expectation.\n"
      - source_book: Algorithms to Live By — Brian Christian & Tom Griffiths
        text: "As Keynes put it, making a crucial distinction between beauty and popularity: professional\ninvestment may
          be likened to those newspaper competitions in which the competitors have\nto pick out the six prettiest faces from
          a hundred photographs, the prize being awarded\nto the competitor whose choice most nearly corresponds to the average
          preferences of the\ncompetitors as a whole. Each competitor has to pick, not those faces which he himself\nfinds
          prettiest, but those which he thinks likeliest to catch the fancy of the other\ncompetitors. We have reached the
          third degree where we devote our intelligences to\nanticipating what average opinion expects the average opinion
          to be.\n"
      - source_book: 'The Great Mental Models, Volume 4: Economics and Art — Shane Parrish'
        text: "Economist John Maynard Keynes used the metaphor of a beauty contest to explain how stock\nprices are set. He
          asked readers to imagine a contest where entrants must choose six faces\nout of a hundred; those who pick the faces
          chosen by the most other people win. Keynes\nbelieved stocks are priced not according to their intrinsic value but
          according to\ninvestors' perceptions of other investors' perceptions — a recursive prediction problem\nwhere the
          fundamental value becomes almost irrelevant to short-term price movements.\n"
    is_convergent: true
    should_extract: true
    expected_fb:
      is_summary: false
      route: FB
      name: Keynesian Recursive Expectation
      definition: "In markets where asset prices depend on collective belief rather than intrinsic value,\nsuccessful prediction
        requires reasoning about what others believe others believe — a\nrecursive chain of mutual expectation. The asset's
        fundamental value decouples from its\nmarket price with each recursion step, as each participant optimizes for the
        anticipated\naverage opinion rather than underlying reality.\n"
      mechanism: "Recursive expectation drives price formation through higher-order belief coordination.\nLevel-1 investors
        value assets on fundamentals. Level-2 investors value based on predicted\nlevel-1 beliefs. Level-k investors value
        based on predicted level-(k-1) beliefs. The\nmechanism amplifies small errors: misperceptions about others' beliefs
        compound with each\nrecursion level, producing price cascades where individually rational decisions generate\ncollectively
        destabilizing speculation. The recursion has no natural termination — at\nsufficiently high k, the fundamental value
        becomes negligible relative to the recursive\nbelief component.\n"
      boundary: "Applies when future prices depend primarily on other investors' behavior rather than\nmeasurable cash flows.
        Fails when: (1) the asset has a contractually guaranteed terminal\nvalue (zero-coupon bond) — the recursion collapses
        to a known endpoint; (2) participants\nhave identical information and reasoning — recursion converges to fundamentals;
        (3)\ntransaction costs exceed speculative margin — the game is not worth playing; (4) a\ndominant player can unilaterally
        set the price — no need to predict others.\n"
      consequence: "Markets governed by recursive expectations exhibit excess volatility relative to\nfundamentals, spontaneous
        coordination on arbitrary focal points (round-number prices),\nand vulnerability to self-fulfilling prophecies where
        collective belief in a price\nmovement causes that movement. This explains why assets with zero cash flows\n(cryptocurrencies,
        collectibles, meme stocks) can sustain high valuations indefinitely\nas long as participants believe others will continue
        to believe.\n"
      evidence_passages:
        - Keynes used the beauty contest as a metaphor for the stock market, where each investor wants to buy the stocks
          that will rise in price, which means the stocks that investors, in general, think will appreciate
        - We have reached the third degree where we devote our intelligences to anticipating what average opinion 
          expects the average opinion to be
        - stocks are priced not according to their intrinsic value but according to investors' perceptions of other 
          investors' perceptions
    rationale: "STEM domain expansion: economics/game theory, sourced from real cluster_90 in the S1.5\nbge-m3 run (3 books,
      cohesion=0.865). Three independent sources (Dixit/Nalebuff,\nChristian/Griffiths, Parrish) converging on Keynes' beauty
      contest mechanism. The\nmechanism explicitly traces recursion levels (1→2→k) and identifies the compounding-error\n\
      dynamic that produces speculative instability — going beyond \"prices reflect beliefs\" to\nexplain WHY recursive belief
      coordination creates excess volatility. The boundary\ndistinguishes speculative from contractual assets as a testable
      condition. This expands\ngolden set domain coverage from psychology/marketing into economics and game theory,\ndemonstrating
      convergent extraction on academic/theoretical source material.\n"
